+    "en": "The laws of counting activity (whole-law-of-counting, counting-under-enclosure) read what is active; this result reads what stands and what depends in the same unfolding. Where nothing is enclosed what stands and what is active satisfy the same laws of counting: embodiment makes them one, and without it both are bound by the tally alone. Under enclosure they part: what the flow takes and gives is standing, so balance and dissipation alone bound the change of what stands, and what embodiment does is carry that bound from what stands to what is active. The counts of dependence are not bound by the premises, since the premises bear on dependence only together with activity, standing and descent: activity needing support asks only that what an active relatum depends on supports it, and bounded transmission only that what a descendant depends on as it comes to stand its parent depended on, within a bound. A reading (an AI argument): what an enclosed unfolding spends is what stands, activity being bound with it only where embodied; this completes the statement of the laws of counting of the theory without the condition, with no axiom, in the three readings, where binding (each chosen premise binding) is stated for activity only. It does not evaluate coming to stand, ceasing to stand, forming or dissolving."
